Since $1\text{ meter}=100\text{ centimeters}$, we know that both $\dfrac{1\text{ meter}}{100\text{ centimeters}}$ and $\dfrac{100\text{ centimeters}}{1\text{ meter}}$ equal $1$.

So we can multiply by either of those fractions to help us convert units, since multiplying by $1$ doesn't change value. In this problem, we multiply by $\blueD{\dfrac{1\text{ meter}}{100\text{ centimeters}}}$ since the quantity we want to convert is in $\text{centimeters}$:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}30\,\cancel{\text{centimeters}}\cdot \,\blueD{\dfrac{1\,\text{meter}}{100\,\cancel{\text{centimeters}}}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{30}{100}\,\text{meters}
\end{align}$
